n experimental system  to test for systemic effects on aging in  different tissues since the circulatory  system is body wide  the earliest work was published in the  late 1950s and early 1960s where here  they could perform the experiments by  combining young and old rats  a more systemic study conducted in 1972  showed that when older members of a  female heterochronic pair outlived  single female controls and homo chronic  female control parabolas  so this hinted that there might be  something in the young blood that was  enabling these older female rats to live  longer  but as correctly pointed out in the  study that increase of life expectancy  is not equivalent to decelerated aging  the younger member of the heterochronic  pair may enhance survival of the older  by protecting it against disease not  directly attributable to senescence  but much of this work has now been  revisited now with newer techniques and  resources  and for a while it was thought that  there must be factors in the young  bloods that have declined in the old  this stemmed from a 2005 publication by  the combo lab where heterochronic  parabiosis ameliorated  phenotypes and in a study in 2020  showing that young blood plasma  fractions were first epigenetic asian  rats  so with these tools we can now see age  reversal  it may be a relief to hear that in this  latter paper they didn't have to fuse  the circulatory system together  and another approach that also avoids  this parabiosis problem is plasma  dilution  this builds on the idea that the  benefits from heterochronic parabolosis  may have also come from effectively  diluting the bad factors in the old  blood  and work again coming from the combo lab  showed this in 2020 whereby diluting  aged plasma and mice with saline and  albumin rejuvenated the three tissue  lines  so they saw signatures of cell  replication which more closely matches  what are seen in younger mice there is  still more data to collect though to  really evaluate if this is age reversal  but since replacement of blood plasma is  already something pretty safe so i guess  time consuming in humans it hopefully  shouldn't take too long to see these  results  but it is worth mentioning that it is  very likely that a combination of both  additional factors from young blood and  dilution of old blood is  beneficial but as i said more work would  be needed to tease these two aspects  apart and to further confirm the  presence of a rejuvenating effect  so to the second strategy cellular  reprogramming  now many of you probably associate this  with the yamanaka factors and rightly so  back in 2006 senior yamanaka published  an out landmark study whereby the  introduction of four factors the  yamanaka factors oskm  when expressed and differentiated mouse  cells could reverse them to pluripotent  stem cells  cells that have the potential to divide  continuously but also differentiate  so they were called induced pluripotent  stem cells  hence this is where your programming  comes in you've effectively altered a  cell's identity  and so by measuring the age of these  reprogrammed cells  again here using epigenetic age which is  based on dna methylation marks a  decrease in age was seen  so that's pretty cool we can turn a  specialized cell into something younger  but what does that mean that's just one  cell well a 2020 paper showed that the  use of three yamanaka factors osk  allowed old mice to regain eyesight and  a new preprint has shown that a single  transient expression of oskm and  naturally aged mice reverse aging in the  pancreas liver spleen and blood  i'll cover this work in more detail when  it's officially published so you want to  make sure you subscribe so you don't  miss that  but moreover you can use these induced  pluripotent stem cells to make entirely  new embryos but this latter paper  reinforces how unleashing this  replicated potential also comes with its  own risks cancer development  and so ultimately to get better control  over these exciting mechanisms it is  best to understand how referring the  identity of a cell could reverse age  and can other factors or combination of  factors be found and will that  combination vary depending on tissue  type  and so instead of yamanaka factors you  may now hear them referred to more  generally as reprogramming factors  but small molecules potentially could  also work  and so as you can probably tell there  are many unanswered questions which is  part of the reason why there's been so  much excitement around the outsource  labs announcement and the rise of more  rejuvenation companies  and so the last strategy  understanding the endogenous  now the strategy is a little different  but nonetheless still interesting partly  because there is much still left to  uncover and is the reason why all of us  were young when we were born that is the  natural rejuvenation that must occur  after fertilization to erase damage from  the egg and sperm from our parents that  give rise to our fresh face cells  recent development of the first single  cell epigenetic clock  single cell age has also provided data  now to support a rejuvenation process in  early development  and so we know that this is a  rejuvenating event the question is what  are the molecular mechanisms involved  part of this age referral may come from  telomere extension as there's some  previous work showing that growing mice  from embryonic stem cells that have been  passaged from wall times in culture  which effectively meant that they had  longer telomeres  enabled the elongation of lifespan with  low incidence of disease  but also as you can see in this figure  early developments there is lots of  rapid division and growth which also  means opportunities for damage to get  diluted  so similar to above with the plasma  dilution but on a very different scale  but yeah understanding the mechanism  could be useful down the line  therapeutically  so to quote from this recent review  article  much of the biological underpinnings of  rejuvenation remain enigmatic and the  current side effects induced by some of  these interventions particularly  reprogramming currently prevent their  translational application  so outsource labs and the other  companies have many challenges ahead  will they succeed who knows but what i  am sure of is that we will uncover some  interesting biology along the way  so i hope you've learned something in  this video thank you to my patreon  supporters and thank you for listening  you

